id "1c0ba606-48a9-527b-9766-dd4a1dcdf258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126974 #edb/ref 1080 "{x_{1}(t) = u_{1}(t) - u_{2}(t); x_{2}(t) = u_{2}(t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126974 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126974 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "48ead97a-9c3b-5e1f-bfd6-75b3c8f05a03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127741 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127741 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127741 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "776072e0-7992-59d8-b1c0-3b4dff3f30ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127742 #edb/ref 1080 "D = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127742 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127742 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aeaad254-403f-52b5-96b5-822a86ff2d0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127743 #edb/ref 1080 "D = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127743 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127743 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bcbb1cbb-e8fd-5068-8ebc-8b68d6a96415" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127744 #edb/ref 1080 "D = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127744 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127744 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f793c275-cfb3-5ecf-9c1f-7fbd638aa13c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127745 #edb/ref 1080 "D = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127745 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127745 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0eb87084-e195-528e-b78c-7167174d40ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127746 #edb/ref 1080 "D = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127746 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127746 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "54c5b6b6-1ec5-5ed9-9de1-1c612a1088f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128951 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5605" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128951 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the substitution that will decouple the following system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128951 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0707ce1-a0fe-5749-9742-4768621332a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128951 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's construct the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ for the given system:\n![](images/cd/cd09988206ce7738d335cf25814f9820d5003c286904c8e136e55724f2fc1185.png)\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-3\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\lambda_2=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1 =-3,$ we get \n![](images/9b/9bd0bffeaccd9f7523829b9f385c5e75a25ecd663877f0b5711d271938e484e6.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/06/0683c697abc42c91bc62d5f52aafa1fd7b79cd5156e999b3aac4802d9359773e.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 =1,$ we get \n![](images/81/816a9a9b4948cae2d5a21b6e5ccf80928cadefb4d8a7ca1d333166b2fe66e5ac.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/49/491ab9ee9c59586dbccb6c043a3225adc3ef95496c056d37891594513f1f6069.png)\n\nTherefore, we have ![](images/6c/6c51e641ebd7c4e64366c23aa9c7e091c3b711724069b47b104dc6f1c588f7bc.png)\n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by $\\mathbf{x}(t) = E\\mathbf{u}(t),$ or equivalently,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)=4{u}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)=3{u}_{1}(t)+{u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128952 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5606" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128952 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{1}(t)+7{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)-4{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=3.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128952 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58c1b360-e67c-5074-919b-18fb5dbab146" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128952 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ for the given system:\n\n![](images/39/39f5b156a53392174c4b0c490f01c7bb3fee6530fc5f1e2e16be43d0de99067b.png)\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-1\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\lambda_2=3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1 =-1,$ we get \n![](images/62/62cc97bb07ba88adc4b9fc6b42f33c924bf8d1d38c38ecf29973264c6ac24ee2.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/96/96afd68567ff82d1a461ad2b38f9492f644982f7dc4628526945bd619335c5ff.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 =3,$ we get \n![](images/27/27f4874dc20e09331a89c5135dcacff5d6cce93d6040fdac954845478324b58b.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/6d/6d3732ee67364278140c7e9ba5ec791cef69f4a60c8533d1f528c4bb5e718a35.png)\n\nThus, the general solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128982 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5974" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128982 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ![](images/72/72d318ad7ca3ea0dff8b598a3c7126fd48a48f25fb6bdfec859e91701ffb9d15.png) and ![](images/75/75c63b77244e0233dd11874e55a1ab674f6149d49b8fdd3c939afa1cf016ca6e.png) be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=2,$ respectively.\nWhat is the general solution of the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128982 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7bde1c8-25e8-5573-9ac4-feedd40e122f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128982 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}, {\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129305 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129305 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the General Solution Given the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of the Corresponding Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129305 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129305 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "16d1e71b-6eda-5c91-81a0-15a1aa979932" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129306 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129306 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129306 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the General Solution to a Linear System of Differential Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129306 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129306 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9cfc838b-2e21-5021-8afc-000d12acdf39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129307 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129307 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129307 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129307 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Decoupling Substitution of a Linear System of Differential Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129307 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129307 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2a962d5e-5cc9-530e-9f95-85c399af7e4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129398 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129398 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129398 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129398 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129398 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129398 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129398 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129398 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs With Distinct Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129398 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186130278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129398 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129398 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "edadecea-96b5-58a0-937e-a3136a7e0eb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129581 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129581 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129581 #edb/ref 1069 "q-324455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129581 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129581 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the substitution that will decouple the following system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129581 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c347aa2-fe8e-5437-94e5-a44fd44640d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129581 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we have $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by $x(t)=Eu(t)$, or equivalently,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129710 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129710 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39052" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129710 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$. Find the decoupling matrix of the differential equation $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129710 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "30504f26-ffc2-5a3c-bc4c-1549ba4e1d3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130278 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130278 #edb/ref 1061 540572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130278 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd45b863-00a5-58fa-8428-a9a8120bd4ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130278 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130279 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130279 #edb/ref 1061 540573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130279 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c550f4f1-3cf8-524d-8169-2af08dc4a285" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130279 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130280 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130280 #edb/ref 1061 540576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130280 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8e58211c-b5cb-545e-80a7-711cd519848c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130280 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130281 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130281 #edb/ref 1061 540579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130281 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5f88f9eb-8194-50fe-b904-394d292db648" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130281 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130282 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130282 #edb/ref 1061 540580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130282 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bdfa89d7-8a8f-598a-98e1-507a34d62369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130282 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130283 #edb/ref 1061 540583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130283 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e1277628-257e-5f4b-942e-3126a1882b65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130283 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130359 #edb/ref 1064 "We've seen how to solve a decoupled system of differential equations. Our goal now is to understand in more detail why this method works. \n\nLet's consider our general linear system of two differential equations once again:\n\n![](images/86/8687a97b82b11a0cfba98e18b2c92e0fbd75cea22f6005109ab43e96d5f5a89e.svg)\n\nWe will show that if the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, we can convert it into an equivalent decoupled system.\n\nIf $A$ is diagonalizable, it has $2$ linearly independent eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}.$ We use these to form the *change of variables matrix* $E,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBecause the columns are linearly independent, $E$ is invertible. Then, the substitution  \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = E\\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwill transform our original system of differential equations into a decoupled one, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) & =Ax(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(Eu(t)) & =A(Eu(t)) \\\\ E{u}^{'}(t) & =AEu(t) \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & ={E}^{-1}AE\\,u(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis a diagonal matrix, and ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1},$ ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ are the eigenvalues of $A$ corresponding to ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}.$\n\nFollowing these steps, we now have the *decoupled system*\n![](images/9e/9e2207c7f946cc4f15141c868c943ddd560aa5e6898ea3ecdd985dcea059e620.svg)\n\nHere, the matrix $D = E^{-1}$ is sometimes called the **decoupling matrix**.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130359 #edb/ref 1065 3650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130359 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "375f9ca0-335a-5289-80aa-053eba96a1f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130359 #edb/ref 1067 "Decoupling a Linear System of Differential Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130393 #edb/ref 1064 "We know how to solve a system of differential equations when they're decoupled. However, most systems are not decoupled. So, how do we solve them?\n\nLet's consider a general linear system of two differential equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)=a\\,{x}_{1}(t)+b\\,{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)=c\\,{x}_{1}(t)+d\\,{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt turns out that if the matrix $A$ has two *distinct real eigenvalues* $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, then $A$ is diagonalizable and we can solve the system.\n\nIf the eigenvectors corresponding to $\\color{red}\\lambda_1$ and $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2$ are ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2},$ respectively, then the general solution to our original system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nThis result is true for any $n \\times n$ system with $n$ distinct eigenvalues. We'll prove this result for a $2\\times 2$ system at the end of the lesson.\n\nNow, let's see some concrete examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130393 #edb/ref 1065 4183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130393 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ffec0b5f-817b-58dc-b521-20db431e3ea6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130393 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130394 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the $2\\times 2$ matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that $A$ is diagonalizable and has eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ with corresponding eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2},$ respectively.\n\nWe've been using the general solution formula \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from? First, remember that the substitution\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ntransforms the matrix differential equation into the equivalent decoupled system\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can solve this new decoupled system using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $\\mathbf{x}(t)=E\\mathbf{u}(t)$, we can transform the system back to the variable $\\mathbf x(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =Eu(t) \\\\  & =E({c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}E[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}E[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our original system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130394 #edb/ref 1065 4184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130394 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "91036007-f4d2-5e97-ac9f-760e367f3217" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130394 #edb/ref 1067 "The Proof of the Formula For a General System" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186299941 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186299941 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186299941 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "024debe0-5fb3-5d92-b541-9a194a2b2146" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300652 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300652 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300652 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "891f8a2d-5509-5159-b687-e6c372ceeab5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300696 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300696 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300696 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9283efc0-a1cb-5b14-aa96-6829ea180b52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300905 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300905 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300905 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bc927e09-c086-5ed6-a3ce-9e4f8fed4928" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301172 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301172 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301172 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f59d8bbc-9194-5ce5-acfc-e3800e7b28b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301499 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186299941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301499 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301499 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301499 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301499 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186301172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301499 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301499 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301499 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186300696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301499 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "53cd92a2-c50d-591c-a4c9-7497aca4b5dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302377 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302377 #edb/ref 1069 "q-327974" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302377 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302377 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-6{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302377 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e5119bc-30ba-5ab9-9edb-058e36254603" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302377 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345432 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345432 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345432 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a8bfb79-cfc3-5844-8f84-8e979ddac966" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345492 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345492 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345492 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "108ce4cb-6473-5d9b-8e0e-8975aff13639" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345776 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345776 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345776 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2fecb22e-9b78-5fb4-99aa-45ef0f590541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346345 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346345 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346345 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "70f3cbf1-3fa6-5f08-928e-38af5abbd951" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347373 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347373 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347373 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e58a7f26-eb29-5b9c-af1f-17e0f7406a7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347693 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186345432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347693 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186345492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347693 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186345776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347693 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347693 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186347373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347693 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347693 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347693 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186347373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347693 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "34381abf-8ba3-5bc9-9655-c2fea1d1a76d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348258 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348258 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79672" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348258 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348258 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ respectively. What is the general solution of the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348258 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6bfac1d8-b297-5aeb-80ab-63193055e75b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348258 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402029 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402029 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402029 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402029 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402029 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402029 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402029 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402029 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186402032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402029 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d6c339bd-cf81-5ede-a22f-a63cc71054fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402030 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)-2{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)=3{u}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402030 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402030 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7a797ac7-33c4-512f-90f8-59eb16783f56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402031 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)=-{u}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402031 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402031 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "54d062ef-f5a7-554f-9aec-32b3e4d45203" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402032 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402032 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402032 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a9817443-5f2f-5697-b9b4-facc30ec893f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402033 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)=2{u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)=2{u}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402033 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402033 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bb06d79b-0719-5c04-a4f5-69705749bb3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402034 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)+{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402034 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402034 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a0f2d3e6-cd5c-5ab7-a220-8d6563dfe260" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
